In article <1991Jun5.032547.16252@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG> xanthian@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG (Kent Paul Dolan) writes:
>There _are_ no undeserved ways to die
>in nethack; nethack is _about_ dying
>in new and frustrating ways.  What
>there are in nethack is undeserved
>successes.

I'm sorry, but I simply can't agree with your perspective on the game, Kent.

NetHack's reputation, as I understand it, is that of being "tough but fair", 
not "out to get the player at all costs". Within the limits of hardware and 
software, and the established mythology which the game employs, the game goes 
OUT OF ITS WAY to avoid killing the player in stupid, unexpected ways. 

If you are frustrated by your deaths, then that is par for the course - it's
part of the learning process. I also frequently find the game - or, more 
correctly, -particular- games that I play - frustrating in the EXTREME. But, 
in those cases, I try to separate _design intentions_ from their occasional 
emotional side-effects.

